\label{ch:ir-design}

The intermediate representation (IR) is the heart of a compiler. It is the language in which programs are analyzed, transformed, and optimized before being lowered to machine code. The design of an IR determines the expressiveness of the optimizer, the ease of implementing analyses, and the quality of the generated code. This chapter explores the fundamental design principles that guide the construction of intermediate representations.

