%% Section 53.1
%% ============================================================
\section{What Makes a Good IR?}\label{sec:good-ir}

An IR must balance competing requirements: it must be close enough to the source to preserve semantic information, yet close enough to the target to enable efficient code generation.

\subsection{Design Criteria}\label{sec:design-criteria}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Expressiveness.} The IR must be able to represent all programs in the source language without loss of information relevant to optimization.
  \item \textbf{Analyzability.} The IR should make program properties (data flow, control flow, dependencies) explicit and easy to analyze.
  \item \textbf{Transformability.} Optimizations should be expressible as local, composable transformations on the IR.
  \item \textbf{Verifiability.} It should be possible to verify that transformations preserve semantics.
  \item \textbf{Efficiency.} The IR should be compact and fast to traverse, enabling rapid compilation.
  \item \textbf{Portability.} The IR should be target-independent, allowing the same optimizer to serve multiple backends.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The IR Spectrum}\label{sec:ir-spectrum}

Compilers typically use multiple IRs, progressing from high-level (close to source) to low-level (close to target):

