\section{SSA vs. Non-SSA}\label{sec:ssa-vs-non-ssa}

The choice between SSA and non-SSA form is one of the most consequential IR design decisions.

\subsection{Non-SSA Form}\label{sec:non-ssa}

In non-SSA form, variables can be assigned multiple times:

\begin{verbatim}
  x = 1
  if (cond) {
    x = 2
  }
  y = x + 1
\end{verbatim}

The value of $x$ at the use site depends on the control flow path. Analyzing such code requires dataflow analysis to determine which definitions reach which uses.

\subsection{SSA Form}\label{sec:ssa-form}

In SSA form, every variable is assigned exactly once. Phi functions merge values at control flow join points:

\begin{verbatim}
  x1 = 1
  if (cond) {
    x2 = 2
  }
  x3 = phi(x1, x2)  // x3 = x1 if we came from above, x2 if from the then-branch
  y = x3 + 1
\end{verbatim}

SSA form makes def-use chains explicit, enabling many optimizations to be expressed as simple local transformations.

\subsection{Comparison}\label{sec:ssa-comparison}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{SSA vs. non-SSA}\label{tab:ssa-comparison}
\begin{tabular}{p{3cm}p{5cm}p{5cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Property} & \textbf{Non-SSA} & \textbf{SSA} \\
\hline
Definitions & Multiple assignments & Single assignment \\
Def-use chains & Implicit (dataflow) & Explicit \\
Phi functions & None & At join points \\
Optimization style & Dataflow-based & Local rewriting \\
Construction & Direct translation & Requires algorithm \\
Code size & Smaller & Larger (phi functions) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{insight}
SSA form is now the dominant IR representation in production compilers (LLVM, GCC, HotSpot, V8). Its key advantage is that it makes data dependencies explicit, transforming global dataflow analyses into local pattern matching. The cost---phi functions and the construction algorithm---is modest and well-understood.
\end{insight}

