\section{Graph vs. Tree vs. Linear Representations}\label{sec:graph-tree-linear}

IRs can be organized as trees, graphs, or linear sequences, each with different trade-offs.

\subsection{Tree Representation}\label{sec:tree-rep}

A tree representation uses a hierarchical structure where each node has a single parent:

\begin{verbatim}
  BinOp(+)
  ├── BinOp(*)
  │   ├── Var(x)
  │   └── Const(2)
  └── Const(3)
\end{verbatim}

\textbf{Advantages:} Simple, easy to traverse, natural for expression trees.\\
\textbf{Disadvantages:} Cannot represent shared subexpressions or cyclic data flow.

\subsection{Graph Representation}\label{sec:graph-rep}

A graph representation allows nodes to have multiple parents, enabling sharing and cycles:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Data-flow graph (DFG).} Nodes are operations; edges are data dependencies.
  \item \textbf{Control-flow graph (CFG).} Nodes are basic blocks; edges are control flow.
  \item \textbf{Program dependence graph (PDG).} Combines data and control dependencies.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Advantages:} Can represent sharing (common subexpressions), cycles (loops), and complex dependencies.\\
\textbf{Disadvantages:} More complex to traverse and transform; requires care to maintain invariants.

\subsection{Linear Representation}\label{sec:linear-rep}

A linear representation is a sequence of instructions:

\begin{verbatim}
  t1 = x * 2
  t2 = t1 + 3
  store t2, [ptr]
\end{verbatim}

\textbf{Advantages:} Simple, compact, easy to serialize.\\
\textbf{Disadvantages:} Ordering constraints can obscure optimization opportunities; must be augmented with CFG information for control flow.

