\section{Multi-Level IR}\label{sec:multi-level-ir}

Modern compilers use multiple IR levels, each suited to different kinds of optimization.

\subsection{The LLVM Approach}\label{sec:llvm-approach}

LLVM uses three IR levels:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{High-level (pre-SSA).} Used for source-level optimizations (inlining, constant folding).
  \item \textbf{Mid-level (LLVM IR).} SSA-based, target-independent. Used for most optimizations.
  \item \textbf{Low-level (SelectionDAG / MachineInstr).} Target-specific. Used for instruction selection, register allocation, and scheduling.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{The MLIR Approach}\label{sec:mlir-approach}

MLIR (Multi-Level Intermediate Representation) takes the multi-level approach to its logical conclusion: it provides a framework for defining custom IR \emph{dialects}, each suited to a particular domain (tensors, quantum circuits, graph neural networks). Dialects can be lowered incrementally, preserving structure at each level.

\subsection{The Graal Approach}\label{sec:graal-approach}

GraalVM uses a sea-of-nodes IR (see \cref{ch:graph-ir}) that combines data and control flow in a single graph. The IR is used for both high-level optimizations (inlining, partial escape analysis) and low-level optimizations (register allocation, code generation).

