\section{IR for Different Purposes}\label{sec:ir-purposes}

Different compilation tasks require different IR properties.

\subsection{IR for Optimization}\label{sec:ir-optimization}

An optimization-friendly IR should:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Make dependencies explicit (SSA form).
  \item Support local transformations (pattern matching, rewriting).
  \item Enable global analyses (dataflow, alias analysis).
  \item Preserve enough information for verification.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{IR for Verification}\label{sec:ir-verification}

A verification-friendly IR should:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Have a formal semantics (operational or denotational).
  \item Support proof-carrying code or certified transformations.
  \item Make invariants explicit (types, effects, memory safety).
\end{itemize}

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Checking an IR Transition with Two Interpreters}

A small reference interpreter is useful at an abstraction boundary
where syntactic comparison no longer makes sense. For instance,
lowering a structured conditional to labelled jumps changes the
shape of the program without changing the value it computes.
Consider a deterministic, first-order subset with fixed-width
integers, a return value, and a defined trap outcome. Implement one
interpreter for the structured IR and another for its linear form.
Give the runner a finite suite of typed input tuples and a separate
step budget for each interpreter: a node in the higher level may
expand into several lower-level instructions.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Structured term $H$, lowered code $L$, test inputs $I$
\State $U\gets[\,]$
\ForAll{$x\in I$}
  \State $h\gets\Call{RunStructured}{H,x}$
  \State $l\gets\Call{RunLinear}{L,x}$
  \If{either run timed out}
    \State append $x$ to $U$ \Comment{Time budget is not semantics}
  \ElsIf{$\mathrm{observe}(h)\ne\mathrm{observe}(l)$}
    \State \Return $(x,h,l)$ \Comment{Keep the concrete counterexample}
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State \Return no mismatch on completed runs, and list $U$
\end{sudocode}

Let $H$ return 0 if $x=0$ and otherwise return $12/x$,
using integer division. On $x=3$ both interpreters should return 4.
On $x=0$ the structured run returns 0; if the lowered code
computes the quotient before the conditional jump, the linear run
instead traps. The runner reports input 0 and the two outcomes.
It does not compare instruction counts or intermediate temporaries,
which have no counterpart at the other level.

The runner's invariant is that every processed input not in $U$
has matched on the shared observations, unless it already returned
a counterexample. The interpreter work is bounded by the sum of
the test budgets. Passing only means agreement on this suite, not
equivalence over all inputs. In a larger IR, decide up front whether
stores, output, and trap reasons are observable. Reusing the
lowerer's implementation inside the reference interpreter can hide
the same bug on both sides.

\subsection{IR for Code Generation}\label{sec:ir-codegen}

A code-generation-friendly IR should:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Be close to the target architecture (instruction set, registers, memory model).
  \item Support instruction selection (tree or DAG matching).
  \item Encode scheduling constraints (latencies, resources).
\end{itemize}

