\section{Case Studies}\label{sec:ir-case-studies}

\subsection{LLVM IR}\label{sec:llvm-ir}

LLVM IR is a typed, SSA-based, RISC-like instruction set. Key features:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Typed.} Every value has a type (integers, floats, pointers, vectors, structs).
  \item \textbf{SSA.} Every virtual register is assigned exactly once.
  \item \textbf{Infinite virtual registers.} Register allocation is deferred to the backend.
  \item \textbf{Target-independent.} The same IR serves x86, ARM, RISC-V, and other targets.
\end{itemize}

\begin{verbatim}
  define i32 @add(i32 %a, i32 %b) {
  entry:
    %sum = add i32 %a, %b
    ret i32 %sum
  }
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{MLIR}\label{sec:mlir-case}

MLIR extends the LLVM approach with a framework for defining custom dialects. Each dialect defines its own operations, types, and attributes. Dialects can be lowered to other dialects, enabling multi-level optimization.

\begin{verbatim}
  // Affine dialect: loop nests with affine bounds
  affine.for %i = 0 to %N {
    affine.load %A[%i] : memref<100xf32>
  }

  // Lowered to standard dialect:
  scf.for %i = %c0 to %N step %c1 {
    %v = memref.load %A[%i] : memref<100xf32>
  }
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Graal IR}\label{sec:graal-ir}

Graal uses a sea-of-nodes IR where all operations (including control flow) are nodes in a graph. The graph is unordered until scheduling, allowing optimizations to reorder operations freely.

\subsection{V8 Ignition/TurboFan}\label{sec:v8-ir}

V8 uses multiple IRs:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Bytecode (Ignition).} A compact, stack-based bytecode for the interpreter.
  \item \item \textbf{TurboFan IR.} A sea-of-nodes graph for the optimizing compiler.
\end{itemize}

The TurboFan IR represents both JavaScript semantics and machine-level operations in a single graph, enabling aggressive optimizations like speculative inlining and hidden class optimization.

\begin{historical}
The evolution of IR design reflects the growing complexity of both source languages and target architectures. Early compilers used a single IR (often a simple three-address code). The development of SSA form in the 1980s~\cite{cytron1991} revolutionized optimization by making data dependencies explicit. The rise of multi-core processors and specialized hardware (GPUs, TPUs) has driven the development of multi-level IRs like MLIR~\cite{lattner2021}, which can represent domain-specific abstractions while still lowering to machine code. The tension between expressiveness (for high-level optimization) and concreteness (for code generation) continues to drive innovation in IR design.
\end{historical}

