\section{Advanced Multi-Level IR and Case Studies}\label{sec:multi-level-advanced}

\subsection{Multi-Level IR Design Patterns}\label{sec:multi-level-patterns}

Modern compilers use multiple IR levels, each designed for specific optimization tasks. The design of each level reflects its position in the compilation pipeline.

\begin{definition}[IR Level Properties]\label{def:ir-level}
Each IR level is characterized by:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Abstraction level.} How close to the source or target.
  \item \textbf{Type system.} The types available at this level.
  \item \textbf{Control flow.} Structured (loops) vs. unstructured (branches).
  \item \textbf{Memory model.} Abstract (objects) vs. concrete (addresses).
  \item \textbf{Optimization focus.} What optimizations are most effective at this level.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Multi-level IR design: typical levels and their properties}\label{tab:multi-level-design}
\begin{tabular}{p{2cm}p{2.5cm}p{2.5cm}p{2.5cm}p{2.5cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Level} & \textbf{Types} & \textbf{Control Flow} & \textbf{Memory} & \textbf{Key Opts} \\
\hline
Source & Generics, ADTs & Structured & Objects & Inlining \\
High IR & Monomorphized & Structured & Objects & Specialization \\
Mid IR & Primitives & SSA & Abstract & CSE, DCE \\
Low IR & Machine types & CFG + SSA & Addresses & Regalloc \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{The LLVM Multi-Level Approach}\label{sec:llvm-multi}

LLVM uses three main IR levels:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Level 1: Clang AST (source-like)
  // - Preserves C/C++ semantics
  // - Supports source-level warnings and errors
  // - Used for template instantiation and macro expansion

  // Level 2: LLVM IR (mid-level)
  // - SSA form, typed, target-independent
  // - Infinite virtual registers
  // - Used for most optimizations

  // Level 3: MachineInstr (low-level)
  // - Target-specific instructions
  // - Physical register names
  // - Used for register allocation and code generation
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{The Swift IR Approach}\label{sec:swift-ir}

Swift uses a multi-level IR with SIL (Swift Intermediate Language) as its primary IR:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{SIL (high).} Preserves Swift-specific features: generics, value types, optionals, ARC.
  \item \textbf{SIL (low).} After generic specialization, ARC optimization, and devirtualization.
  \item \textbf{LLVM IR.} Final lowering to target-independent LLVM IR.
\end{itemize}

The key insight of SIL is that Swift-specific optimizations (ARC optimization, generic specialization, existential opening) must happen before lowering to LLVM IR, because LLVM IR cannot represent Swift's type system features.

\subsection{The Graal/Truffle Approach}\label{sec:graal-truffle}

GraalVM uses a \emph{sea-of-nodes} IR where all operations (including control flow) are nodes in a single graph:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Sea-of-nodes representation:
  // Nodes: Phi, Add, Load, Store, If, Return, ...
  // Edges: data flow (solid) and control flow (dashed)

  // Example: x = a > 0 ? a + 1 : a - 1
  //   cond = CmpGT(a, 0)
  //   if (cond) {
  //     true_val = Add(a, 1)
  //   } else {
  //     false_val = Sub(a, 1)
  //   }
  //   x = Phi(true_val, false_val)

  // The sea-of-nodes allows reordering:
  // - Add and Sub can be moved before the If (speculative)
  // - If the branch is predictable, only one path executes
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Case Study: JavaScript Compilation in V8}\label{sec:v8-case-study}

V8 compiles JavaScript through multiple IR levels:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Parsing.} Source code $\to$ AST (with scope information).
  \item \textbf{Ignition.} AST $\to$ bytecode (stack-based, for the interpreter).
  \item \textbf{TurboFan.} Bytecode $\to$ sea-of-nodes graph (for the optimizing compiler).
  \item \textbf{Maglev.} An additional mid-tier compiler for faster optimization.
  \item \textbf{Code generation.} Graph $\to$ machine code.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{V8's compilation tiers}\label{tab:v8-tiers}
\begin{tabular}{p{2cm}p{2.5cm}p{2.5cm}p{2.5cm}p{2.5cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Tier} & \textbf{IR} & \textbf{Speed} & \textbf{Quality} & \textbf{Use} \\
\hline
Ignition & Bytecode & Fastest & Lowest & Initial execution \\
Maglev & Mid-level & Fast & Medium & Warm-up \\
TurboFan & Sea-of-nodes & Slow & Highest & Hot code \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Case Study: Rust's MIR}\label{sec:rust-mir-case}

Rust uses MIR (Mid-level Intermediate Representation) as its primary IR:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{HIR (High-level IR).} Close to the AST, preserves generics and trait bounds.
  \item \textbf{MIR.} CFG-based, with explicit borrows and drops. Used for borrow checking and most optimizations.
  \item \textbf{LLVM IR.} Final lowering for code generation.
\end{itemize}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Rust MIR example:
  fn add(x: i32, y: i32) -> i32 {
    bb0: {
      _0 = Add(_1, _2);   // _0 = return, _1 = x, _2 = y
      return;
    }
  }

  // MIR preserves borrow information:
  fn borrow_example(x: &mut i32) {
    bb0: {
      _2 = &_mut(*_1);    // reborrow
      _3 = *_2;            // read through borrow
      StorageDead(_2);     // borrow ends here
    }
  }
\end{verbatim}

