\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:summary-53}

The design of an intermediate representation is one of the most consequential decisions in compiler construction. The IR must balance expressiveness, analyzability, transformability, and efficiency. The choice between SSA and non-SSA, graph and linear, high-level and low-level, determines the kinds of optimizations that can be performed and the quality of the generated code.

Modern compilers use multiple IRs, each suited to a different phase of compilation. The trend toward multi-level IRs (MLIR) reflects the growing diversity of both source languages and target hardware.

For further reading, we recommend Muchnick's comprehensive treatment~\cite{muchnick1997}, the LLVM language reference~\cite{llvm2024}, and the MLIR overview by Lattner et al.~\cite{lattner2021}.
